// Adaptation of Alice's Adventures in Wonderland by Lewis Carroll, first
// published in 1865. This branching story is original connective prose; only
// short phrases are echoed from the public-domain text. The situations come
// from Chapter III, "A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale", and Chapter VIII, "The
// Queen's Croquet-Ground".

VAR prizes = 0
VAR visits = 0
LIST pocket = ["comfits", "thimble"]
FLAG gave_thimble = false
FLAG spoke_for_gardeners = false
STATE race = unrun [unrun, running, finished]

=== bank ===
SET visits = visits + 1
The pool has drained to a ring of wet grass, and the birds and beasts stand
about in draggled feathers, cross and uncomfortable.
{visits > 1:
    The Dodo looks up as if it has been waiting for you all along.
- else:
    The Mouse clears its throat and offers to make everyone dry.
}
* [Join the Caucus-race]
    SET race = running
    Nobody says go, so everyone simply begins.
    -> race
* [Ask what a Caucus-race is]
    The Dodo explains that the best way to explain it is to do it.
    SET race = running
    -> race
+ [Stand still and watch the others]
    You count the Lory's feathers until the party grows impatient.
    -> bank

=== race ===
The course is a loose circle marked in the turf, and the runners start and
stop whenever the mood takes them.
* [Stop and ask who has won]
    SET race = finished
    -> prizes
+ [Run one more circle]
    Your feet make a soft, wet sound on the grass.
    -> race

=== prizes ===
{race == finished:
    The Dodo sits with one finger pressed to its forehead and thinks.
}
At last it announces that everybody has won, and all must have prizes.
* [Hand round the comfits] {if "comfits" in pocket}
    REMOVE pocket, "comfits"
    SET prizes = prizes + 1
    The large birds complain that they cannot taste theirs.
    -> thimble
* [Offer the thimble] {if "thimble" in pocket}
    REMOVE pocket, "thimble"
    SET gave_thimble = true
    -> thimble
+ [Ask the Mouse for its tale instead]
    The Mouse sighs and says its tale is long and sad.
    -> bank

=== thimble ===
{prizes > 0:
    The Dodo thanks you gravely for the comfits.
- else:
    The Dodo waits, and the party waits with it.
}
* [Accept the thimble as your own prize] {if gave_thimble}
    The Dodo begs your acceptance of this elegant thimble, and everyone cheers.
    -> garden
* [Bow and say nothing]
    You bow as solemnly as you can manage.
    -> garden
+ [Ask the Mouse for its tale instead]
    The Mouse is already walking away, and the Crab's daughter is scolded.
    -> bank

=== garden ===
A rose-tree stands by the garden door, its white roses half painted red.
Three gardeners in flat oblong coats drop their brushes and bow low.
* [Ask why the roses are being painted]
    Two whispers that the tree ought to have been red, and that heads are at
    stake if the Queen notices.
    -> croquet
* [Warn the gardeners that the Queen is coming]
    SET spoke_for_gardeners = true
    They scatter, and the procession arrives in a long ribbon of cards.
    -> croquet

=== croquet ===
The Queen asks whether you can play croquet, and the soldiers wait for an answer.
{spoke_for_gardeners:
    Three of them have hidden themselves in a flowerpot at your suggestion.
- else:
    The gardeners lie face down, hoping to be mistaken for the pack.
}
* [Say yes, and take up a flamingo mallet]
    The flamingo twists its neck round to look at you.
    -> heads
* [Step back towards the garden door]
    You keep your eyes on the Queen and move one slow pace at a time.
    -> waking

=== heads ===
The game begins with everyone playing at once, and the Queen shouts for heads
about once a minute. Your flamingo refuses to hold still, the hedgehog rolls
under a rose-bush, and you decide that you have had enough of croquet.
-> END

=== waking ===
You reach the door and the garden softens at the edges, like a picture held
too close to the eye. Somewhere above you a voice is saying it is time to
wake up, and the croquet-ground folds away.
-> END
